# Line endings.
#
# Git stores everything here with LF, but a Windows checkout with core.autocrlf=true
# rewrites the working tree to CRLF. That is harmless for source that only ever runs
# through a compiler — and fatal for anything the SERVER executes directly, because the
# deployment tarball is built from the working tree, not from the object store.
#
# The failure it produced, on 2026-08-13: the backup script reached the server with CRLF,
# so the CR became part of the interpreter path in its shebang and systemd reported
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
# with exit code 127. The unit was installed, enabled and scheduled, and would have failed
# silently every night at 02:30 until someone needed a restore.
#
# Marking these `eol=lf` keeps the working tree LF on every platform, so what gets packaged
# is what the server can run.

*.sh        text eol=lf
*.service   text eol=lf
*.timer     text eol=lf
*.conf      text eol=lf

# Kept binary so git never touches their bytes.
*.jks       binary
*.keystore  binary
*.apk       binary
*.png       binary
*.jpg       binary
*.ico       binary
